the index.\nL1125@P36: • Index An information book is more useful if it has an index.\nL1126@P36: This is usually an alphabetical list of subjects, people and other\nL1127@P36: important items that are written about in the book, each with a\nL1128@P36: page reference. An index is usually at the back of a book. The\nL1129@P36: page references make it easy for the user to find the information\nL1130@P36: they want. For example, use the index in this book to find out\nL1131@P36: where there is more information about different types of stock.\nL1132@P36: After the word 'stock', you will find several numbers. These\nL1133@P36: numbers direct you to the pages in the book which have\nL1134@P36: information about stock printed on them. Make sure your\nL1135@P36: students do not confuse an index with a glossary (or key words\nL1136@P36: section), which lists difficult or foreign words and explains what\nL1137@P36: they mean, but does not give page references.\nL1138@P36: Before students use the library, check that they know what is the\nL1139@P36: front cover, the back cover and the spine; how to tell the title from\nL1140@P36: the author (this can be quite difficult for students}; who the\nL1141@P36: publisher is; where to find the publication date and where to find\nL1142@P36: your own library details, like the return date label.\nL1143@P36: Teaching tip\nL1144@P36: Your students may be set a project that asks them one\nL1145@P36: question about how to grow rice, one question on how to\nL1146@P36: make aeroplanes and one question on Nelson Mandela's\nL1147@P36: work in South Africa.\nL1148@P36: Explain that if they want quick answers to these\nL1149@P36: questions they need to find a reference book and use the\nL1538@P47-48: register, as shown in the first part of figure 6.1. This is the book's accession number.\nL1539@P48: Be sure to write it in the book.\nL1540@P48: Inside front cover\nL1541@P48: Figure 6.1 Making an entry in an accession register\nL1542@P48: Write the\nL1543@P48: accession\nL1544@P48: number on the\nL1545@P48: top right-hand\nL1546@P48: side of the title\nL1547@P48: page or first page\nL1548@P48: of the book.\nL1549@P48: Return date label\nL1550@P48: glued into the\nL1551@P48: book\nL1552@P48: 4 Take any library book. This will be number 1 in your accession\nL1553@P48: register. Write down the book's title, author and publisher in\nL1554@P48: the columns beside the accession number. Keep the details brief\nL1555@P48: as other information, such as the publication date, ISBN and\nL1556@P48: number of pages, can be entered on the title catalogue cards\nL1557@P48: and shelf list cards. (You will learn about these in chapter 8.)\nL1558@P48: S Write the book's accession number inside the book. A good\nL1559@P48: place to write this number is on the top right-hand side of the\nL1560@P48: first page. It is best to write all accession numbers in the same\nL1638@P50: 50 mm and glue it\nL1639@P50: to itself to make a\nL1640@P50: pocket. (If you are\nL1641@P50: using the exercise\nL1642@P50: or borrower cards\nL1643@P50: system you do not\nL1644@P50: need this pocket,\nL1645@P50: so do not fold up\nL1646@P50: the page.)\nL1647@P50: When you have made the\nL1648@P50: return date label, put glue on\nL1649@P50: a 2-3 em strip at the top and\nL1650@P50: then stick it on to the book's\nL1651@P50: title page, or the first page\nL1652@P50: after the inside front cover.\nL1653@P50-51: Figure 6.2 How to make a school nameplate and return date label 45 46\nL1654@P51: If you have access to a stencil this might save you time, as\nL1655@P51: Christine George, working in Sierra Leone, found:\nL1656@P51: 'Return date labels can also be made by typing a stencil, which takes\nL1657@P51: about nine labels, and have them run off on duplicating paper.'\nL1658@P51: Teaching\nL1659@P51: tip\nL1660@P51: It is important to process new books as soon as you can.\nL1661@P51: If your school library is sent a large number of books at\nL1662@P51: the same time, it may be useful to keep two or three of\nL1663@P51: them off the shelves in order to show the next group of\nL1664@P51: student library monitors or club members exactly how to\nL3025@P87: Every book needs a book pocket and a book ticket. The book\nL3026@P87: pocket can be made of strong paper, and you can make it at the\nL3027@P87: same time as you make the school's nameplate. Alternatively, you\nL3028@P87: can buy book pockets from library suppliers.\nL3029@P87: If you are making separate book pockets, then glue them at the\nL3030@P87: front of the book on the title page. You do not need a lot of glue to\nL3031@P87: stick them into the book- just paste the glue on a thin 25 mm (1\nL3032@P87: inch) strip at the top of each book pocket. Some people like to glue\nL3033@P87: them on the inside back cover; there is no right or wrong way, but\nL3034@P87: make sure you are consistent. Look back at chapter 6, and figure\nL3035@P87: 6.2, for more details about making and sticking book pockets.\nL3036@P87: Next you need to make a book ticket from strong card. The ticket\nL3037@P87: should be tall enough to be seen when it is inside the book pocket:\nL3038@P87: a recommended size is 6 em x 10 em (2I/2 x 4 inches). A good way\nL3443@P98: jacket or front cover and in the front of the book on the title\nL3444@P98: page. If students can learn to identify these parts of the book\nL3445@P98-99: with no problems, this will be a great help to you. 93 94\nL3446@P99: • Point out the school's name and the return date label, which\nL3447@P99: tells students when books that they borrow should be returned\nL3448@P99: to the library.\nL3449@P99: • Explain that books are fragile. Show students how to turn pages\nL3450@P99: and explain the importance of using bookmarks. Tell students\nL3451@P99: that they should always have clean hands when handling books,\nL3452@P99: and if they borrow a book they must keep it in a clean, dry and\nL3453@P99: safe place.\nL3454@P99: • Show students an information (non-fiction) book. Point out the\nL3455@P99: contents page. Explain that it is usually at the front of the book\nL3456@P99: and that it lists the main subjects the book's author has written\nL3457@P99: about. Show students the index. Explain that it is usually at the\nL3458@P99: back of the book and that it lists key words and topics alongside\nL4892@P138: have found on the beach.\nL4893@P138: Thesaurus A reference book of related word lists. It can help\nL4894@P138: users increase-their vocabulary.\nL4895@P138: Title The name of a book.\nL4896@P138: Title catalogue All the titles of the books in the library, written\nL4897@P138: down on individual index cards, then filed in alphabetical order.\nL4898@P138-139: 133 134\nL4899@P139: Title page The page at the front of a book which has the title,\nL4900@P139: author and publisher printed on it. It is usually a right-hand page\nL4901@P139: and is where you are recommended to glue the school nameplate\nL4902@P139: and return date label.\nL4903@P139: Title verso page A left-hand page near the front of a book\nL4904@P139: containing information about the book (e.g. publisher, date\nL4905@P139: published, etc.)\nL4906@P139-140: . ' Index\nL4907@P140: •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL4908@P140: Note: page numbers in italic include photographs and\nL4909@P140: illustrations\nL4910@P140: accession borrower cards 45, 66, culture 8, 37, 40, 102,\nL4911@P140: numbers 42, 59, 61, 68, 78,80,81,129 104\nL4912@P140: 63, 64, 70, 78 borrowing 12, 128, 129\nL4913@P140: register I 0, 40, 42, see also lending; rules daily routine 108,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://files.peacecorps.gov/documents/ED204_Setting-Up-and-Running-a-School-Library.pdf)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"pattern\":\"publisher\"}); Total lines: 5122\nL1016@P33: Top of book\nL1017@P33: Bottom of book\nL1018@P33: Figure 4.1 Learning about the parts of a book\nL1019@P33: The parts of a book\nL1020@P33: Publisher\nL1021@P33: Title on the\nL1022@P33: front cover\nL1023@P33: Front cover\nL1024@P33: Author's name\nL1025@P33: on front cover\nL1026@P33: • Cover This can be hard (hardback) or soft (paperback). The\nL1027@P33: cover helps protect the book. Some teacher-librarians like to put\nL1028@P33: an extra cover, which they buy from library suppliers or make\nL1029@P33: from strong paper or sticky-back plastic, on popular books.\nL1030@P33: • Front cover This may have a picture on it and usually has the\nL1031@P33: title of the book and the author's name; it may also have the\nL1032@P33-34: publisher's name. • Back cover This often has a summary of what the book is\nL1033@P34: about; or it may have some people's comments about how much\nL1034@P34: they enjoyed the book- these are called reviews. Most back\nL1035@P34: covers list the book's own internationally recognised ten-digit\nL1036@P34: ISBN, usually nowadays in the form of a computer readable bar\nL1037@P34: code. An ISBN is a computer number used by publishers and\nL1038@P34: booksellers to identify a title. In some large bookshops you will\nL1039@P34: be able to order the books you want if you give the bookseller\nL1040@P34: just this number. However, it is not essential information for\nL1041@P34: libraries without computers.\nL1042@P34: • Spine This is the backbone of a book. If it breaks, or is\nL1043@P34: damaged, there is a risk that the book's pages will fall out. On\nL1044@P34: the spine most books usually have the title of the book and the\nL1045@P34: author as well as a symbol (picture or letters) which identifies the\nL1046@P34: publisher.\nL1047@P34: • Spine label All the books in your library should have a spine\nL1048@P34: label, glued or stuck to the bottom of the spine. This will be used\nL1049@P34: by students to locate books they want and to find out if the book\nL1050@P34: is information or fiction; it will also give a visual reminder of\nL1051@P34: what the book is about. If, for example, a student is looking for a\nL1052@P34: biology book, he or she (depending on your classification\nL1053@P34: system) would look for green spine labels or the classification\nL1054@P34: code number 500. (See chapter 7 for more information about\nL1055@P34: classification systems.)\nL1056@P34: • Title page This is usually a right-hand page near the front of\nL1057@P34: the book. On it will be the book's title, author and publisher.\nL1058@P34: • Title verso page This is traditionally the left-hand page\nL1059@P34: immediately after the title page. On it will be more information\nL1060@P34: about the publisher (for example, the publisher's address) and\nL1061@P34: about the book (what year it was published, if it has been\nL1062@P34: reprinted and who printed it). The title verso page will also have\nL1063@P34: details of the book's copyright.\nL1064@P34: • School nameplate and return date label It is recommended\nL1065@P34: that you glue your school nameplate and a return date label on\nL1066@P34: to the first right-hand page as you open the book (see figure 4.3).\nL1067@P34: Explain to students that this shows who owns the book and that\nL1068@P34: it is also the place to find out what day they should return the\nL1069@P34: book if they have borrowed it from the library.\nL1070@P34-35: 29 30\nL1071@P35: Contents page\nL1072@P35: <\nL1073@P35: J ---\nL1074@P35: ... _--- - - <<\nL1075@P35: s_ -- _ - - 41\nL1076@P35: :r,.. .... , ............. so\nL1077@P35: de~~: ............. b.z\nL1078@P35: Title verso page\nL1079@P35: Look at the title verso page to find\nL1080@P35: out which company published the\nL1081@P35: book and when.\nL1082@P35: Put the return date label on the first page\nL1083@P35: after the inside front cover. If it has the\nL1084@P35: book's title and the author's name\nL1085@P35: printed on it, then this is the title page.\nL1086@P35: - '\"\nL1087@P35: Figure 4.2 Learning about the inside of a book\nL1088@P35: Inside front cover\nL1089@P35: Inside back cover\nL1090@P35: Look for the\nL1091@P35: index at the\nL1092@P35: back of\nL1093@P35: the book.\nL1094@P35: Pages\nL1095@P35: Title page\nL1096@P35: Title page with\nL1097@P35: return date label\nL1098@P35: stuck on top of it\nL1099@P35: Pages\nL1100@P35: Book's accession\nL1101@P35: number\nL1102@P35: Glue thin strip\nL1103@P35: School's name\nL1104@P35: - F\"T::J_Jil---- Columns to write\nL1105@P35: The school\nL1106@P35: nameplate is\nL1107@P35: folded up lj3 to\nL1108@P35: make a card\nL1109@P35: pocket.\nL1110@P35: or stamp the\nL1111@P35: date by which\nL1112@P35: the borrower\nL1113@P35: must return the\nL1114@P35: book to the\nL1115@P35: library\nL1116@P35-36: Figure 43 Where to place the return date label in a book • Contents page Most information books have a contents page.\nL1117@P36: This gives an outline, or sometimes a brief summary, of what will\nL1118@P36: be in each chapter- and a page number so that you can turn\nL1119@P36: straight to the right chapter. For example, in a book on the\nL1120@P36: lifecycle of a butterfly you might see from the contents page that\nL1121@P36: chapter 1 is on larvae, chapter 2 is on the chrysalis, chapter 3 is\nL1122@P36: on caterpillars and chapter 4 is on butterflies. If you want more\nL1123@P36: detailed information then you should look at both the contents\nL1124@P36: page and the index.\nL1125@P36: • Index An information book is more useful if it has an index.\nL1126@P36: This is usually an alphabetical list of subjects, people and other\nL1127@P36: important items that are written about in the book, each with a\nL1128@P36: page reference. An index is usually at the back of a book. The\nL1129@P36: page references make it easy for the user to find the information\nL1130@P36: they want. For example, use the index in this book to find out\nL1131@P36: where there is more information about different types of stock.\nL1132@P36: After the word 'stock', you will find several numbers. These\nL1133@P36: numbers direct you to the pages in the book which have\nL1134@P36: information about stock printed on them. Make sure your\nL1135@P36: students do not confuse an index with a glossary (or key words\nL1136@P36: section), which lists difficult or foreign words and explains what\nL1137@P36: they mean, but does not give page references.\nL1138@P36: Before students use the library, check that they know what is the\nL1139@P36: front cover, the back cover and the spine; how to tell the title from\nL1140@P36: the author (this can be quite difficult for students}; who the\nL1141@P36: publisher is; where to find the publication date and where to find\nL1142@P36: your own library details, like the return date label.\nL1143@P36: Teaching tip\nL1144@P36: Your students may be set a project that asks them one\nL1145@P36: question about how to grow rice, one question on how to\nL1146@P36: make aeroplanes and one question on Nelson Mandela's\nL1147@P36: work in South Africa.\nL1148@P36: Explain that if they want quick answers to these\nL1149@P36: questions they need to find a reference book and use the\nL1538@P47-48: register, as shown in the first part of figure 6.1. This is the book's accession number.\nL1539@P48: Be sure to write it in the book.\nL1540@P48: Inside front cover\nL1541@P48: Figure 6.1 Making an entry in an accession register\nL1542@P48: Write the\nL1543@P48: accession\nL1544@P48: number on the\nL1545@P48: top right-hand\nL1546@P48: side of the title\nL1547@P48: page or first page\nL1548@P48: of the book.\nL1549@P48: Return date label\nL1550@P48: glued into the\nL1551@P48: book\nL1552@P48: 4 Take any library book. This will be number 1 in your accession\nL1553@P48: register. Write down the book's title, author and publisher in\nL2628@P76: start with A will be put on the left-hand side of the top shelf.\nL2629@P76: Remember to keep the spines facing out and the writing on them\nL2630@P76: easy to read. This means the title, author and publisher's symbol\nL2631@P76: will be running from the tqp of the spine to the bottom. Students\nL2632@P76: may need plenty of practice arranging books on shelves before\nL2633@P76: they are confident about doing it correctly.\nL2634@P76: As one VSO teacher in Kenya found:\nL2635@P76: 'I have spent the past year trying to teach students that books are placed\nL4730@P134: usually at the front of information books and is a useful way of\nL4731@P134: finding out if the book you are looking through has the\nL4732@P134: information you want.\nL4733@P134: Copy In a library, a copy means one book. If you have several\nL4734@P134: books, with the same title and by the same author, then you have\nL4735@P134: multiple copies. Each copy should be given its own unique\nL4736@P134: accession number.\nL4737@P134: Copyright A legal term which protects writers' work from\nL4738@P134: reproduction for a specific number of years without the permission\nL4739@P134: of the publisher.\nL4766@P135: cupboards in a formroom if the school does not have a separate\nL4767@P135: rQom for a library.\nL4768@P135: Front cover Protective cover of a book. Most have the book's\nL4769@P135: title written on them as well as the names of the author and\nL4770@P135: publisher.\nL4771@P135: Genre Fiction books of the same type: for example, many\nL4772@P135: authors specialise in writing one type of book such as mysteries,\nL4773@P135: romance, or thrillers.\nL4774@P135: Hardback A book with a strong cover, usually more expensive\nL4775@P135: and not as easily damaged as a paperback book.\nL4833@P137: information. Posters can be made by the library club, or bought\nL4834@P137: from shops, to decorate the library.\nL4835@P137: Project box/subject file A box or file in which a variety of stock\nL4836@P137: about the same subject, e.g. the weather, is kept. A project box is a\nL4837@P137: good place to store pamphlets and videos which might not be\nL4838@P137: easy to see on bookshelves.\nL4839@P137: Publication date The date when a book is published. It is usually\nL4840@P137: at the front of the book.\nL4841@P137: Publisher A company that produces books or magazines.\nL4842@P137: Quarterly A way of describing something that is done four times\nL4843@P137: a year. Some magazines, for example, are published every three\nL4844@P137: months ~ four times a year.\nL4845@P137: Readathon A fun story-telling exercise when a series of students\nL4846@P137: read a book, out loud, for a long time.\nL4847@P137: Reference book An information book where students and staff\nL4848@P137: can find quick answers, and which cannot be borrowed from the\nL4849@P137: library.\nL4850@P137: Reprint A further edition of a book, produced by the publisher\nL4851@P137: when the initial number of copies printed has sold out.\nL4852@P137: Return date label A place in the book where the date by which a\nL4853@P137: borrowed book must be returned to the library is written or\nL4854@P137: stamped.\nL4855@P137: Routine A task that is done regularly.\nL4856@P137: Shelf guide A sign that shows library users where books and\nL4857@P137: stock are kept on the bookshelves.\nL4858@P137: Shelf list A complete list of all the books and stock in the library,\nL4859@P137: kept in the same order as the books on the bookshelves.\nL4884@P138: e.g. for six months or one year. Most subscription offers will save\nL4885@P138: you some money in the long term.\nL4886@P138: Textbook A standard book for a particular subject of study.\nL4887@P138: Theme table A table used for special displays on one topic. The\nL4888@P138: theme 'What do we find on the beach', for example, might include\nL4889@P138: a map of the local beach, pictures of special seabirds, some shells,\nL4890@P138: dried seaweed, a turtle egg and a project book from one of the\nL4891@P138: classes with short stories and pictures of strange objects students\nL4892@P138: have found on the beach.\nL4893@P138: Thesaurus A reference book of related word lists. It can help\nL4894@P138: users increase-their vocabulary.\nL4895@P138: Title The name of a book.\nL4896@P138: Title catalogue All the titles of the books in the library, written\nL4897@P138: down on individual index cards, then filed in alphabetical order.\nL4898@P138-139: 133 134\nL4899@P139: Title page The page at the front of a book which has the title,\nL4900@P139: author and publisher printed on it. It is usually a right-hand page\nL4901@P139: and is where you are recommended to glue the school nameplate\nL4902@P139: and return date label.\nL4903@P139: Title verso page A left-hand page near the front of a book\nL4904@P139: containing information about the book (e.g. publisher, date\nL4905@P139: published, etc.)\nL4906@P139-140: . ' Index\nL4907@P140: •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL4908@P140: Note: page numbers in italic include photographs and\nL4909@P140: illustrations\nL4910@P140: accession borrower cards 45, 66, culture 8, 37, 40, 102,\nL4911@P140: numbers 42, 59, 61, 68, 78,80,81,129 104\nL4912@P140: 63, 64, 70, 78 borrowing 12, 128, 129\nL4913@P140: register I 0, 40, 42, see also lending; rules daily routine 108,\nL4914@P140: 43-6, 47, 49, 70, 109, British Council 114 109-10\nL4915@P140: 110, Ill, 128 browser boxes 22, 23, 33, damage 10, 18, 39, 108\nL4916@P140: accounts 25-6 98, 129 avoidance of 85-91\nL4917@P140: African Books Collective budgets see resources and handling 28, 32,\nL4918@P140: Ltd 116 87\nL4919@P140: aid agencies 112-14, 117 and insects 86-7\nL4920@P140: AIDS disease 40 catalogues penalties 12, 77, 129\nL4921@P140: alphabetical order 106, author 58, 67 and repair 3, 13, 14,\nL4922@P140: 129 book 115-16, 117, 130 38, 76, 89, 110, Ill\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view1\",\"pattern\":\"verso\"}); To